<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simón Patiño</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simón Iturri Patiño was a Bolivian mining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> known as the 'King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">Tin</a>'. From the La Salvadora mine at Uncía, struck around 1900, he built a company that controlled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>'s tin output and acquired smelters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. By the 1920s he was among the richest men in the world, and he lived mostly abroad until his death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Patiño's biographers disagree about his early life. Many wrote that he was a cholo of mixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> and Spanish descent, born to a poor mother, while his authorised biography held that he was of European ancestry alone and the son of a provincial leader. He was in fact the illegitimate son of Julio Abasto and María Patiño of Cochabamba. Before entering mining he either managed a store in Oruro or spent years in private schools.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Patiño worked for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> company Compañía Huanchaca de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> and then for Fricke y Compañía, collecting debts for its store. In 1894 he accepted the deed to a rocky mountainside in settlement of a debt of $250 owed by a prospector, and was dismissed for taking worthless property; legend has it that he had to repay the store himself. The first years of work yielded little, but the rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> vein found in 1900 changed his fortunes.</p><p>Over the following decade he gained control of nearby mines and of other important Bolivian mines, among them Catavi, Siglo XX, Uncia and Huanuni. He bought out Chilean interests in his company, acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> smelters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and in 1924 merged his Bolivian mining and railway assets into Patiño Mines &amp; Enterprise Consolidated Inc., registered in Delaware. By the 1940s he controlled the international tin market, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he was believed to be one of the five wealthiest men in the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Patiño had divided his time between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> since about 1912, and after a heart attack in 1924 he settled abroad, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. While in Paris he served as Bolivia's minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and represented it at the Évian Conference in 1938. He married Albina Rodriguez, with whom he had five children. He was buried in a white mausoleum in the province of Cochabamba. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivian-national-revolution">Bolivian Revolution of 1952</a> confiscated the Patiño mines.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simón Iturri Patiño” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From about 1900 tin replaced silver as the main export, making the mine owners <b>Simón Patiño</b>, Mauricio Hochschild and the Aramayo family the most powerful men in the country and turning mining camps such as Catavi and Siglo XX into centres of militant unionism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cochabamba, in what is now <a href="/countries/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-17.39,-66.16&amp;year=1916" aria-label="Cochabamba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2753 2809 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Simón Patiño"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3203 3079h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3203 3079h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-17.39,-66.16&amp;year=1916">What was here in 1916? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
